<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paraguayan War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Paraguayan War, fought from 1864 to 1870, was a conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and the Triple Alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. It ended in Paraguay's defeat and catastrophic loss of population. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> it was a victory financed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> revenues, but it turned the army into a professional force whose officers later overthrew the monarchy in 1889.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war.jpg" alt="Paraguayan War" width="297" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Inkratt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Escenas_de_la_Guerra_de_la_Triple_Alianza.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Since independence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the Spanish-American countries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> had been troubled by territorial disputes, because the old Tordesillas line of 1494 did not match the actual occupation of the land. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> region, Brazil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> struggled for hegemony. Brazil recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>'s independence in 1844, when Argentina still regarded it as a breakaway province. Brazil intervened in unstable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> in 1851, 1855 and again in 1864, against Atanasio Aguirre; this last intervention led to the war. The traditional view holds that Paraguay's president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-solano-lopez">Francisco Solano López</a> used the Uruguayan War as a pretext to gain control of the Platine basin, but the causes are also attributed to border conflicts and to Brazilian and Argentine meddling in Uruguay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> began with over 60,000 trained men, 400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and a squadron of steamboats. Paraguay took the initiative by invading the Brazilian province of Mato Grosso on 14 December 1864, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a> and the Argentine province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrientes">Corrientes</a>. By the end of 1865 the Triple Alliance was on the offensive, and its armies invaded Paraguay in April of the following year.</p><p>On 10 October 1866 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> gave unified command to the Marquess of Caxias, who in the Pikysyry campaign took the army through the swamps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-chaco">Gran Chaco</a> to cross behind the fortified Paraguayan line, leading to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>. Defeated in conventional warfare, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> conducted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance that further destroyed its army and civilians. The Count of Eu led the Campaign of the Hills from 1869, including the battles of Piribebuy and Acosta Ñu, where more than 5,000 Paraguayans died. López was killed by Brazilian forces at Cerro Corá on 1 March 1870.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Casualty figures are hotly disputed. Estimates of the share of its population that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> lost range from 7 percent to 69 percent, and disease, hunger and exhaustion caused most of the deaths. Among the Brazilians, disease killed more than wounds did, and some historians think cholera was a leading cause. Paraguay lost about 140,000 square kilometres of disputed territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and was occupied until 1876, and its large war debt was forgiven in 1943 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a>.</p><p>The war brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> to its peak of influence, but its public debt and the social crisis that followed are regarded as crucial factors in the fall of the Empire and the proclamation of the First Brazilian Republic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paraguayan War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By mid-century Brazil was producing around half of the world's coffee, and the proceeds financed the modernisation of Rio de Janeiro, the first railways, beginning with the Mauá line of 1854, and the military effort that sustained the <b>Paraguayan War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the last major engagement of the <b>Paraguayan War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-16">16 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
